    "20": 0.0116,
    "30": 0.0014,
    "50": 0.0001,
    "note": "The true (Raccess Exact) accessibility itself is rarely large at large w: 80.2% of cells reach P_u>=0.01 at w=1, versus 0.01% at w=50. Consistent with the mechanistic explanation: a long completely-unpaired run has low Boltzmann weight (fewer base pairs) in the true ensemble too, and beam pruning disproportionately drops the already-rare states needed to track it as w grows."
  },
  "conclusion": "Raising loop caps alone (100/60 vs 100/30) is not enough to make LinearRaccess accurate at w approaching or exceeding the default cap; beam width is the binding constraint, and doubling it (100->200) cuts median relative error 3- to 31-fold depending on w. LinearRaccess remains substantially more accurate than Raccess Window at every tested w regardless of beam setting.",
